## Why This Is Asked

Feature flags are ubiquitous in modern apps. This question probes practical testing across unit, integration, and end-to-end levels, plus resilience and data-driven CI—key for beginner-friendly interview depth.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and graceful fallback
- Mocking remote services for deterministic tests
- Data-driven CI with user segments
- Distinguishing unit, integration, and end-to-end coverage
- Handling retries and latency in test plans

## Code Example

```javascript
class FlagClient {
  constructor(fetchFn) {
    this.fetchFn = fetchFn;
    this.cache = null;
    this.ts = 0;
  }
  async isEnabled(flag) {
    const now = Date.now();
    if (this.cache !== null && now - this.ts < 60000) return this.cache;
    const val = await this.fetchFn(flag);
    this.cache = val;
    this.ts = now;
    return val;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend tests for multiple flags that might interact?
- How would you measure the cost of flag lookups in CI or production?

## Why This Is Asked

This tests ability to design end-to-end, multi-tenant privacy-conscious tests for streaming data, including isolation, schema evolution, and canary risk.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy isolation
- Data privacy and retention
- Schema evolution and backward compatibility
- Streaming backpressure and performance
- Canary rollout and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: simple data leakage check
function checkIsolation(records) {
  const seenTenants = new Set();
  for (const r of records) {
    if (seenTenants.has(r.tenantId)) return false;
    seenTenants.add(r.tenantId);
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generate tenant-scoped synthetic data with privacy constraints?
- What instrumentation would you add to verify no cross-tenant leakage under burst load?

## Why This Is Asked

Feature flags and canary rollouts are common in production, but testing them across regions with multi-tenant overrides is tricky. This question probes how you design end-to-end tests, ensure isolation, observe, and roll back safely.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flag resolution with per-user and per-tenant overrides
- Canary rollout across multiple regions
- Observability: traces, metrics, and logs
- Rollback safety and data isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton for flag resolution
describe('Flag resolution', () => {
  it('applies user-level overrides and region-specific rules', () => {
    const ctx = {userId:'u1', tenant:'t1', region:'eu-west-1', featureFlags:{newFeature:'on'}};
    expect(resolveFlag(ctx)).toBe(true);
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate a region failure mid-rollout and verify rollback?
- How would you validate no cross-tenant data leakage during experiments?

